        "# In this notebook, I put together a simple end-to-end semantic segmentation\n",
        "# pipeline using a DeepLabV3 model and a custom CUDA kernel.\n",
        "#\n",
        "# I first exported the DeepLabV3 model from PyTorch to ONNX and ran inference\n",
        "# using ONNX Runtime with GPU support. This gave me per-pixel class logits,\n",
        "# which I then passed to a CUDA kernel for post-processing.\n",
        "#\n",
        "# To keep things simple, I assumed a batch size of 1 everywhere.\n",
        "# The CUDA kernel was written to process one image at a time, which is\n",
        "# usually enough for demos and inference. Supporting larger batch sizes\n",
        "# would just need an extra batch dimension in the kernel.\n",
        "#\n",
        "# The segmentation mask was stored as uint8, which worked fine here since\n",
        "# DeepLabV3 outputs only 21 classes. This kept the implementation simple\n",
        "# and memory usage low.\n",
        "#\n",
        "# I resized the input image to a fixed size (520x520) to match what the\n",
        "# model expects, so the ONNX output and the CUDA post-processing stayed\n",
        "# consistent.\n",
        "#\n",
        "# The CUDA kernel handled the argmax per pixel, generated the semantic mask,\n",
        "# and applied it to the input image for a chosen target class.\n",
        "#\n",
        "# At the end, I compared the CUDA results with a simple CPU reference\n",
        "# to make sure both the mask and the masked image matched.\n"
      ]
